<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khengarji I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1548">1548</a>–<a href="/years/1585">1585</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khengarji I was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja</a> ruler who united Cutch in the mid-16th century and is regarded as the founder of the Cutch State. Raised at the court of the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, he overcame rival Jadeja kinsmen with their support, took the title Rao and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a> his capital. His descendants ruled Cutch until its accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1948.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khengarji was the son of Jam Hamirji of Lakhiarviro, chief of one branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja</a> clan and a descendant of Othaji. Jam Rawal, who blamed Hamirji for the murder of his own father, Jam Lakhaji, killed Hamirji in 1524 and ruled Cutch for more than two decades. Khengarji, then 15, and his brother Sahibji were in Ahmadabad and escaped. With the help of his father's sister, a wife of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, he joined the sultan's army and became his confidant, and he is said to have saved the sultan's life by killing a lion during a royal hunt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Asked to name his reward, Khengarji sought help to win back Cutch. In 1538 the sultan gave him 1,000 soldiers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Morbi and the title of Rao. From Morbi he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-rawal">Jam Rawal</a> and won Rapar and the villages around it, welcomed by many as the rightful heir. In 1548, when a large army sent jointly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the sultan came against him, Jam Rawal left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> and founded Nawanagar; in folklore the goddess Ashapura, the clan deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadejas</a>, had told him in a dream to go.</p><p>Khengarji was crowned at Rapar in 1548, and in 1549 he moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a>, a city founded by his father. He united eastern, central and western Cutch, parts of which had been held by Chawdas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathi-people">Kathis</a> as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadejas</a>, and took Santalpur, Chadchat near Palanpur and the estate of Pandu Mehvas. He bound twelve related Jadeja landholding families and two Waghela families to his rule, and in 1580 he founded the port of Mandvi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Khengarji died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a> in 1585. His son Bharmalji I succeeded him on the throne, and another son, Bhojrajji, became one of the Bhayyat with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Kera. The Bhayyat system that Khengarji established gave the descendants of the royal family their own domains within the state, which they held and governed under its feudal order while accepting the ruler's authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khengarji I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several Jadeja branches competed until <b>Rao Khengarji</b> I united the country in the mid-16th century and made Bhuj his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/cutch-state">Cutch State</a></li></ul></section>
